/* Theory — Intro */
"theory.intro.title" = "Neo-Riemannian Theory";
"theory.intro.body" = "Neo-Riemannian theory (NRT) provides tools for analyzing chord progressions that resist explanation through traditional tonal functions. Developed from Hugo Riemann's late 19th-century harmonic writings and expanded by David Lewin, Richard Cohn, and Steven Rings in the 1990s–2000s, NRT describes how major and minor triads relate through minimal voice-leading transformations in a chromatic space without a fixed tonic.\n\nThis approach is especially useful for analyzing passages in Schubert, Brahms, Liszt, Wagner, and Bruckner where chords move by smooth semitone steps but cannot be assigned Roman numerals within a single key.";

/* Theory — Operations */
"theory.ops.title" = "The Six Operations";
"theory.ops.subtitle" = "All NRT operations are involutions: applying the same operation twice returns to the starting chord. They always change the mode of the triad.";

/* Operation short descriptions (shown collapsed) */
"op.l.short" = "Keeps the minor third. Moves the root by semitone.";
"op.p.short" = "Keeps the fifth. Changes mode (same root).";
"op.r.short" = "Keeps the major third. Moves by whole tone.";
"op.n.short" = "Inverts the triad. Two voices move by semitone.";
"op.h.short" = "Hexatonic pole. No common tones. Maximum distance.";
"op.s.short" = "Keeps the third. Two voices move by semitone.";

/* Operation long descriptions (shown expanded) */
"op.l.long" = "L (Leading-tone exchange) keeps the minor third of the triad and converts the major third into a perfect fourth by displacing one note by a semitone. Applied to a major triad, the root descends a semitone. Applied to a minor triad, the fifth ascends a semitone. The name refers to the behavior of the leading tone in traditional tonal voice-leading. L and P together generate the hexatonic cycles.";

"op.p.long" = "P (Parallel) keeps the perfect fifth and changes the mode, moving the third by a semitone. It connects a major triad to its parallel minor (same root), and vice versa. This is the simplest of the NRT operations — only one voice moves, by a single semitone. In the Cube Dance, P connects adjacent nodes within a hexatonic cycle.";

"op.r.long" = "R (Relative) keeps the major third and converts the minor third into a perfect fourth. It connects a major triad to its relative minor (and vice versa), displacing one voice by a whole tone. R is one of the two operations (with N) that preserve a Weitzmann region. Alternating R and N generates all six chords of a region.";

"op.n.long" = "N (Nebenverwandt, or 'next related') inverts a major triad around its root, and a minor triad around its fifth. Two voices each move by a semitone. Like R, it preserves the Weitzmann region. The operation reveals a symmetrical relationship between triads: C major and F minor are both inversions of each other under N, differing only in which note serves as the axis.";

"op.h.long" = "H (Hexatonic Pole) is a compound operation equivalent to PLP or LPL. It maps a triad to the most distant chord within its hexatonic cycle — the only chord with no notes in common. All three voices move by a semitone. In the circular Cube Dance diagram, the hexatonic poles appear diametrically opposite one another. There are three pole pairs in each cycle: {C, A♭m}, {E, Cm}, {A♭, Em}.";

"op.s.long" = "S (Slide) keeps the third and displaces the other two voices by a semitone each, equivalent to the compound chain RNR (or NRN). It connects chords of opposite mode that share a third but differ on all other notes. David Lewin named it 'slide' for the chromatic slippage it produces — a gesture common in the music of Schubert and his successors.";

