/* Theory — Hexatonic */
"theory.hex.title" = "Hexatonic Cycles";
"theory.hex.subtitle" = "Generated by alternating L and P.";
"theory.hex.body" = "An LP chain generates a closed cycle of six triads — three major and three minor — before returning to the starting chord. There are exactly four such cycles, which together exhaust all 24 major and minor triads. Adjacent chords within a cycle share two common tones and differ by a single semitone displacement.\n\nThe three major triads in each cycle have roots a major third apart, forming an implicit augmented triad. Operations that preserve the hexatonic cycle: L, P, H.";

/* Theory — Weitzmann */
"theory.weitz.title" = "Weitzmann Regions";
"theory.weitz.subtitle" = "Generated by alternating R and N.";
"theory.weitz.body" = "Carl Friedrich Weitzmann observed in 1853 that each augmented triad is symmetrically surrounded by six consonant triads: move any of its three notes up a semitone to get a minor triad, or down a semitone to get a major triad. This gives six triads per augmented chord — a Weitzmann region.\n\nThere are four regions, one per augmented triad, and together they cover all 24 triads. Each triad belongs to exactly one hexatonic cycle and exactly one Weitzmann region. Operations that preserve the Weitzmann region: R, N, S.";

